"Let $G$ be a graph with $V\\geq 9$ vertices. A $(G,k)$-cockade is obtained recursively by starting with a graph isomorphic to $G$ and repeatedly identifying complete subgraphs of size $k$ of two such cockades. For graphs $K_{a_1,\\ldots,a_r}$, the notation denotes the complete multipartite graph with parts of sizes $a_1,\\ldots,a_r$.\n\n**K10-minor extremal graph conjecture.** A graph on $V\\geq 9$ vertices with no $K_{10}$-minor has at most $8V-36$ edges, unless it is isomorphic to one of the following graphs: a $(K_{1,1,2,2,2,2,2},7)$-cockade; $K_{1,2,2,2,3,3}$; $K_{2,2,2,2,2,3}$; $K_{2,2,2,2,2,3}$ with an edge deleted; $K_{2,3,3,3,3}$; $K_{2,3,3,3,3}$ with an edge deleted; $K_{2,2,3,3,4}$; or the graph obtained from the disjoint union of $K_{2,2,2,2}$ and $C_5$ by adding all edges joining them.\n\nThis conjecture generalizes the known extremal results for graphs with no $K_8$- or $K_9$-minor, identifying the possible exceptional graphs in the $K_{10}$-minor case. Its resolution is not supplied in the source."